From: eirikr <iamhumanipromise1010101@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 21:44:39 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] xip_ssi: treat an address word that has not arrived yet
 as a wait

ProcessReceive moves to State.Address when it dequeues the instruction word.
The CPU writes the instruction and the address to DR0 as two bus writes, and
the clocking thread can tick between them, so the Address branch can find
the FIFO empty. The state holds and the next tick retries, which is the
ordinary case, but the branch logged it at Error level and a guest driver's
log fills with faults that are nothing of the kind. It logs at Noisy now,
like the Instruction branch does for the same condition.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (claude-opus-5)
---
 emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_xip_ssi.cs | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_xip_ssi.cs b/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_xip_ssi.cs
index 8a06b3e..31da11b 100644
--- a/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_xip_ssi.cs
+++ b/emulation/peripherals/spi/rp2040_xip_ssi.cs
@@ -360,7 +360,12 @@ namespace Antmicro.Renode.Peripherals.SPI
               lock (fifoLock) { got = transmitBuffer.TryDequeue(out data); }
               if (!got)
               {
-                this.Log(LogLevel.Error, "Requested address transmission, but there is no data in FIFO");
+                // The instruction word arrived on one tick and the address
+                // word has not been written yet: the clocking thread has run
+                // ahead of the CPU, as it does whenever a bus write lands
+                // between two ticks. The state holds and the next tick
+                // retries, so this is a wait rather than a fault.
+                this.Log(LogLevel.Noisy, "Address transmission requested before the address reached the FIFO, waiting");
                 return;
               }
 
